WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.949
You know that moment : the one when you're
watching a great movie, and you're taken out

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:06.680
of it every time a particular actor shows
up onscreen?

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.400
A single person can throw a whole movie out
of whack with a performance that's clearly

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.990
disconnected from everything else.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.700
It can be painful to watch.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.430
But the true test of a great movie is whether
or not it can withstand that one clunker.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:23.330
Here's a look at some great movies that were
almost ruined by one bad performance.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.000
Pacific Rim

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.630
Pacific Rim seemed like a "sure thing" in
Hollywood.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.220
It's basically Transformers vs. Godzilla,
only smarter, and it had visionary director

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.890
Guillermo del Toro behind it.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.860
But there are no certainties in Hollywood.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.050
Del Toro simply didn't do the math when he
decided not to cast a bona fide movie star

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.120
in the lead role.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.690
Instead, he brought in Sons of Anarchy star
Charlie Hunnam, hoping that he'd rise to the

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.800
occasion and be the charismatic, thoughtful
and romantic tough guy the film needed.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.710
But Hunnam didn't rise.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.230
His performance is flat and lifeless.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:59.280
He also had zero chemistry with co-star Rinko
Kikuchi and Pacific Rim nearly crumbled under

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.950
the weight of his blandness.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.210
However, the movie didn't totally crumble.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.970
It's still a lot of fun to watch, and it made
a lot of money overseas.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:10.190
But we can only wonder how much better it
might've been if Hunnam matched the rest of

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.330
the energy in the movie.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.640
Gangs of New York

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.000
It's easy to blame a bad performance on the
actor who delivered it.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.460
But sometimes it's all the result of poor
casting, and Cameron Diaz was definitely wrong

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.710
for Martin Scorsese's 19th century turf-war
drama Gangs Of New York.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:30.170
To be fair, Diaz was on a roll at the time,
having scored accolades for Being John Malkovich

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:31.180
and Vanilla Sky.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.700
But Gangs of New York is a very different
animal.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.610
Playing pickpocket Jenny Everdeane required
Diaz to dye her hair red and don a Scottish

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:39.610
accent.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:40.610
Or maybe it was Irish?

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:44.530
To be honest, it's impossible to tell, and
more often than not, the accent just sort

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:45.520
of disappears.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.580
"Maybe we can pal in together."

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.020
"Oh, I think you're, uh, a bit rough for this
sort of game."

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:51.820
"Besides.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.820
I work alone."

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:57.670
There's also a fundamental disconnect between
Diaz and her character—a tough-as-nails

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:58.670
thief.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:03.050
Diaz never quite finds the character's essence,
and it frequently undermines the film.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.120
"Don't tell me you're angry with me.."

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.120
"No.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.600
I'm through with ya."

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.400
Thank goodness Daniel Day-Lewis was around
to keep things interesting.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.270
Shakespeare In Love

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.720
Back when Shakespeare in Love somehow bested
Saving Private Ryan to win the Oscar for best

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:22.310
picture in 1999, lots of critics thought it
was a clever and romantic take on the life

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.260
of history's most beloved playwright.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.050
But they probably also would've mentioned
how weird it is when Ben Affleck turns up

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.670
as legendary English actor Ned Alleyn.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:34.900
"What is the play and what is my part?"

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.360
Affleck's appearance in the film is a genuine
head-scratcher.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:42.270
We're still not sure how director John Madden
allowed him to go with that horrible British

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.270
accent.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.890
"Pay attention.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.730
You will see how genius creates a legend!"

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.410
But Affleck makes everything even worse by
trying to bury it under a smarmy layer of

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:52.470
machismo.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.140
It doesn't work, and it leaves us utterly
disliking a character that should've been

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:57.370
a lot of fun.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:01.490
The film is almost unwatchable when Affleck
is onscreen and his presence becomes one of

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.840
those glaring distractions that most films
can't overcome.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.700
But Shakespeare In Love still works in spite
of him, which is something we can't say about

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.030
Daredevil.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.420
The Prestige

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:16.380
Many fans of director Christopher Nolan argue
that his 2006 film The Prestige is his best

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:17.380
work.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.099
It's got that mix of high-concept and high-entertainment
fare that Nolan is known for.

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:25.410
But we'd be a little more inclined to call
it his best movie if we could go back in time

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.910
and make him recast the role of Olivia.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:32.110
If you're wondering why Scarlett Johansson
was hired to play a turn-of-the-century Brit,

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:33.300
you're not alone.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.450
It's hard to believe she was Nolan's first
choice for the part, and we can't imagine

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.849
that producers were worried about packing
big stars into their film.

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:44.349
They already had Batman and Wolverine in the
lead roles and Rebecca Hall, Michael Caine

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:46.000
and David Bowie to back them up.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.440
So, why Johansson?

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.770
Well, that remains a mystery.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:52.770
All we know for sure is that she couldn't
handle the British accent or the turn-of-the-century

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:53.770
dialogue.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:57.879
She struggles so much that she seems to forget
there are other actors in her scenes.

00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:02.360
It would take more than magic to look past
ScarJo's bad performance.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:03.950
Inglourious Basterds

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:08.670
Quentin Tarantino's Inglourious Basterds will
be remembered for a lot of reasons.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:13.160
Not only did it rewrite history and give Adolf
Hitler the brutal end he deserved, it also

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.060
made a star out of Michael Fassbender and
brought Christoph Waltz an Oscar.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.229
But while Tarantino has always had a knack
for casting exactly the right person in exactly

00:04:22.229 --> 00:04:26.689
the right part — and resurrecting a career
or two in the process — his decision to

00:04:26.689 --> 00:04:31.430
cast Mike Myers as a high-ranking British
officer was one of those WTF moments we still

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:32.460
can't quite get over.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.569
"Basically we have all our rotten eggs in
one basket."

00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:39.340
"The objective of Operation Keno?

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:41.940
Blow up the basket."

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.540
Not that casting Myers wasn't worth the risk.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:48.319
The Saturday Night Live veteran has proven
that he's capable of doing great things with

00:04:48.319 --> 00:04:52.860
the right part, and Tarantino has proven he
can make almost any risk pay off.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.680
But both of them missed the mark here.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:59.349
Myers seems to be trying so hard not to do
Austin Powers that he ends up just doing a

00:04:59.349 --> 00:05:01.659
watered down version of Austin Powers.

00:05:01.659 --> 00:05:05.659
His appearance in the film — even behind
all that makeup — feels like a stunt.

00:05:05.659 --> 00:05:07.580
"Got the jist?"

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.089
Batman Begins

00:05:09.089 --> 00:05:12.919
Christopher Nolan has clearly had some trouble
casting female roles.

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:17.379
If Scarlett Johansson turning up in The Prestige
didn't hammer that point home, allow us to

00:05:17.379 --> 00:05:21.050
present exhibit B: Katie Holmes in Batman
Begins.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:24.849
We're willing to admit that her role as Bruce
Wayne's childhood sweetheart Rachel Dawes

00:05:24.849 --> 00:05:27.659
isn't exactly the most well-rounded character.

00:05:27.659 --> 00:05:32.490
Rachel is probably the worst-scripted character
in the entire trilogy, and in Batman Begins,

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:35.190
she's the definition of a stock love interest.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.439
In the right hands, characters like Rachel
can be quite memorable.

00:05:38.439 --> 00:05:42.849
Unfortunately, Holmes brought zero nuance
to the character, delivered her lines with

00:05:42.849 --> 00:05:45.809
no emotion, and Rachel always seems two-dimensional.

00:05:45.809 --> 00:05:48.699
That made it easy for Nolan to recast the
character for The Dark Knight.

00:05:48.699 --> 00:05:53.529
And even though Rachel isn't the sharpest
character in Nolan's trilogy, Maggie Gyllenhaal

00:05:53.529 --> 00:05:57.780
managed to do what Holmes couldn't: make Rachel
seem like a real person.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:58.789
Thanks for watching!

